{"type":"message","id":"8a124da5","parentId":"2c297b80","timestamp":"2026-06-04T07:39:20.436Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"read_ts_AY89a18k5D81pyGYj-vkVNPjAzGHvgKpFIC8sLrsE8-sz1MxEn-7aT94D4YpdYAOd9TZ9_Yv0hpQrnmqfqMet_8XTj4jR1Df3RoghW6ChMCSb-XyAVY_AX4lstyisuSrHxfor_rO6w2inai4P6HjK5cbfubpT6LgOCpbRado5Hhy3WbC6xRqaNKgCjrDHWbhOVjgx9x5a4eVH7Ieu-wNSd8gyuMJ2o25l9RhSEZqKT4WX2OvhrpGy_bFEekvXW9BFpPurtsEHltLeroZ2w5V-oI-EV_mihsB-_iT2rheTGkdYjmmbyzAYEpJUU1f6XvWD7UsWWJHh6ILbXs09OlKyn1HgS9zS7fYZSpFQs_g6z1ItJ2rK5siRY2miM0tcarINPnff1eQ3Wczr15qGaxeRch4D21QdKcmhP72C9DjW_EK5Keqbp2HHUWfEg1tdR7-62c0k4x-oATvQA2F2hvfyjYILcBTE4vy7MynI4t30CNXu9UQnAQKo50BIFoQHW_sLsGWDZxxK3aguM13vPLXgclVosgMxI1Cq9fgIWJrQ-DgDvMVY4zSvIHen5fncFan9LfsZ0qit62HkRsoOm8BTTMkfVYgpNudHHCIxiytciVuctwoecIYFjqwaCz0ujesPorFDEh0NpbCgYC323T5dw4NjbCuw9xTWqnT9ILmHZrvxoik9xhKc1X4LhlepRWGt9CCn4DE8McGYil2PvGq3ulugFA_PJCs3eRjf78k4Y2bo5AaOlfdmVwBBDdv33HwxbZibmLCXKfj_1XlGuFAqmCnITLz79usiFAOKXgCP6ryFVreHslQOhcd5e_RlFVxxsXuHK1wMX1IrWQrAAQPn4zJsUbT4nF0rYToyWopHyEfeOZQ2LVbpGwbF70","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"META\n- SEO Title: UK Stablecoin Regulation: Caps Could Choke Market Growth\n- Meta Description: New UK stablecoin regulation proposals from the Bank of England, including a £20,000 holding cap, face criticism for potentially making a pound market uneconomic.\n- URL Slug: uk-stablecoin-regulation-caps-choke-market\n- Categories: Regulation, Stablecoin\n- Primary Keyword: UK stablecoin regulation\n- Secondary Keywords: Bank of England, GBP stablecoin, financial stability\n\n---\n\n# UK Stablecoin Regulation: BoE Caps May Choke Market Before It Starts\n\nThe proposed **UK stablecoin regulation** is facing significant pushback, as lawmakers warn the Bank of England’s stringent proposals could stifle the nascent pound-denominated stablecoin market. A House of Lords committee has urged the central bank to reconsider holding caps and reserve rules that could render GBP stablecoins commercially non-viable from the outset.\n\nAt the heart of the debate are measures designed to protect financial stability that critics argue are too severe for a market that barely exists. The Bank’s plan includes a temporary £20,000 holding limit for individuals and a requirement for systemic issuers to hold 40% of their reserves in non-interest-bearing central bank accounts. According to a recent report from *CryptoSlate*, these rules could undermine the UK's goal of becoming a crypto hub. ([Source](https://cryptoslate.com/bank-of-england-stablecoin-caps-may-choke-the-uks-pound-token-market-before-launch/))\n\n## UK Stablecoin Rules Face Industry Pushback\n\nThe Financial Services Regulation Committee’s June 3 report sharpened the focus on the trade-off between stability and growth. Lawmakers support 1:1 backing but question if the Bank’s specific safeguards are properly calibrated for a nascent market.\n\n### Holding Caps and Reserve Economics Under Fire\nThe two most contentious proposals are the personal holding caps and reserve composition. A £20,000 limit per person could restrict a stablecoin's utility for payments. More critically, requiring issuers to park 40% of backing assets in non-yielding deposits at the Bank of England directly impacts their business model. This split-backing model is designed for liquidity, but the committee warns it could cripple an issuer's revenue.\n\n### A Principles-Based Alternative\nThe Lords committee advocates for a flexible, principles-based approach, suggesting the Bank monitor market development and only impose limits if risks emerge. It also urged the Bank to consider paying interest on required deposits at the Bank Rate. This would prevent a scenario where stringent rules exist but no companies are willing to launch regulated GBP stablecoins.\n\n## The Financial Stability Case for Caution\n\nThe Bank of England’s cautious stance is rooted in the unique structure of the UK’s credit system. It fears a rapid, large-scale shift from bank deposits into stablecoins could destabilize the nation's credit supply.\n\n### Protecting the UK's Credit System\nSarah Breeden, the Bank's deputy governor for financial stability, noted that UK banks provide about 85% of household credit, far higher than the 30-40% in the US. If deposits were to flow en masse into stablecoins, it could cause a significant contraction in credit available for homes and businesses. This potential for a \"digital bank run\" informs the Bank's desire for circuit breakers like holding caps, a defense against a future where stablecoins become primary money for daily transactions.\n\n## A Nascent Market at a Crossroads\n\nThe UK's stablecoin market is still in its infancy, while the global market, estimated at over $310 billion, is dominated by US dollar-pegged tokens like those from Tether and Circle. This global reality creates a strategic dilemma for the UK. A successful sterling stablecoin market could enhance payments, support tokenized settlement, and reduce reliance on dollar-based alternatives.\n\nHowever, if the regulatory framework makes it too difficult or costly to operate, the UK risks ceding this ground entirely. The country is already seen as lagging behind the US and EU in finalizing its crypto regime. The next set of draft rules, expected in mid-2026, will be a critical signal of whether the Bank of England is willing to adjust its firewall to allow a domestic market to flourish.\n\n### The Systemic Threshold Uncertainty\nA key piece of missing clarity is the definition of when a stablecoin becomes \"systemic.\" This threshold determines when an issuer transitions from being regulated solely by the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) to a stricter dual-regulation regime under both the FCA and the Bank of England.\n\nWithout a clear and predictable trigger, scaling a stablecoin business becomes a significant risk. The Lords committee has pressed HM Treasury for more clarity on this point, emphasizing that uncertainty could deter investment.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe debate over the **UK stablecoin regulation** encapsulates the challenge of balancing innovation with financial stability. While the Bank of England's caution is understandable given the UK's credit structure, lawmakers warn that an overly prescriptive rulebook could prevent a domestic GBP stablecoin market from achieving commercial scale. The forthcoming draft rules will reveal whether regulators are prepared to build a framework that not only protects the system but also allows a competitive token economy to thrive.\n\n## FAQs\n**1. What are the most contentious proposals in the UK's stablecoin regulation?**\nThe most debated proposals are the temporary holding limits of £20,000 for individuals and the requirement for systemic stablecoin issuers to hold 40% of their reserves as non-interest-bearing deposits at the Bank of England. Critics argue these measures could make pound-based stablecoins commercially unviable.\n\n**2. Why is the Bank of England concerned about stablecoins?**\nThe Bank's primary concern is financial stability. It fears a rapid shift from bank deposits to stablecoins could disrupt the UK's credit system, where banks provide about 85% of household credit. This could lead to a contraction in lending to households and businesses.\n\n**3. How could the proposed rules impact the UK's competitiveness?**\nIf the rules make it too expensive to issue a regulated GBP stablecoin, innovators and users may default to offshore, dollar-denominated alternatives. This would undermine the UK's ambition to be a global crypto hub and limit the benefits of a domestic tokenized payment system.\n\n**4. What is the difference between a systemic and non-systemic stablecoin?**\nA non-systemic stablecoin is regulated by the FCA. A stablecoin becomes \"systemic\" when it is deemed large enough to pose a risk to the financial system. At that point, it falls under a stricter dual-regulation regime by both the FCA and the Bank of England. The exact threshold remains undefined.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1780558760436}}
